          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %em string Lie 2-algebra
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+object"} infinitesimal approximation
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+2-group"} Lie 2-group
            that is called the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+2-group"} string 2-group
          %p
            It is a shifted
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L-infinity-algebra"} ∞-Lie algebra
            central extension
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                  %mo →
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi b
                  %mi 𝔲
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi 𝔰𝔱𝔯𝔦𝔫𝔤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi 𝔰𝔬
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  0 \to \mathbf{b} \mathfrak{u}(1) \to \mathfrak{string}(n)
                  \\to \mathfrak{so}(n) \to 0
          %p
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra"} Lie algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔰𝔬
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{so}(n)
            by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+2-algebra"} Lie 2-algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi b
                  %mi 𝔲
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{b} \mathfrak{u}(1)
            which is classified by the canonical (up to normalization)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra+cohomology"} Lie algebra 3-cocycle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔰𝔬
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{so}(n)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                  %mo :
                  %mi 𝔰𝔬
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi b
                    %mn 2
                  %mi 𝔲
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mu : \mathfrak{so}(n) \to b^2 \mathfrak{u}(1)
                  \\,.
          %p
            When
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            is normalized such that it represents the image in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deRham+cohomology"} deRham cohomology
            of the generator of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integral+cohomology"} integral cohomology
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi H
                    %mn 3
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Spin
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H^3(X,Spin(n))
            of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Spin+group"} Spin group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+integration"} Lie integration
            of the String Lie 2-algebra is the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/String+Lie+2-group"} String Lie 2-group
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            We spell out first an explicit algebraic realization of the string Lie 2-algebra and then give its abstract definition as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+fiber"} homotopy fiber
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+%E2%88%9E-bundle"} principal ∞-bundle
          %h3#InComponents In components
          %p
            As with any
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L-%E2%88%9E+algebra"} L-∞ algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔰𝔱𝔯𝔦𝔫𝔤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{string}(n)
            equivalently in terms of its
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chevalley-Eilenberg+algebra"} Chevalley-Eilenberg algebra
          %p
            There are various equivalent models we discuss a small one with a trinary bracket, and an infinite dimensional model which is however strict in that it comes from a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+crossed+module"} differential crossed module
          %h4#skeletal_model Skeletal model
          %p
            Write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔤
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %mi 𝔰𝔬
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g} := \mathfrak{so}(n)
            in the following. The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chevalley-Eilenberg+algebra"} Chevalley-Eilenberg algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi CE
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CE(\mathfrak{g})
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
            has a degree 3 element
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mu = \langle -, [-,-]\rangle
                  \\,,
          %p
            well defined up to normalization (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle - ,- \rangle
            is the canonical bilinear symmetric
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/invariant+polynomial"} invariant polynomial
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [-,-]
            the Lie bracket), which is closed in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi CE
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CE(\mathfrak{g})
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mi 𝔤
                  %mi μ
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  d_{\mathfrak{g}} \mu = 0
                  \\,.
          %p
            Hence this is the canonical (up to normalization) 3-cocycle in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra+cohomology"} Lie algebra cohomology
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔤
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
          %p
            The Chevalley–Eilenberg algebra of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔰𝔱𝔯𝔦𝔫𝔤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{string}(n)
            is
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi CE
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔰𝔱𝔯𝔦𝔫𝔤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mo ∧
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mo *
                  %mo ⊕
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mi 𝔰𝔱𝔯𝔦𝔫𝔤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  CE(\mathfrak{string}(n)) = (\wedge^\bullet (\mathfrak{g}^* \oplus \langle b\rangle), d_{\mathfrak{string}})
                  \\,,
          %p where
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi b
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
                is a single new generator in degree 2;
            %li
              %p
                the differental
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi d
                        %mi 𝔰𝔱𝔯𝔦𝔫𝔤
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d_{\mathfrak{string}}
                coincides with
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi d
                        %mi 𝔤
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d_{\mathfrak{g}}
                on
                = succeed ":" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi 𝔤
                          %mo *
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}^*
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi d
                        %mi 𝔰𝔱𝔯𝔦𝔫𝔤
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi 𝔤
                            %mo *
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi d
                        %mi 𝔤
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      d_{\mathfrak{string}} |_{\mathfrak{g}^*} = d_{\mathfrak{g}}
            %li
              %p on the new generator it is defined by
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi d
                        %mi 𝔰𝔱𝔯𝔦𝔫𝔤
                      %mo :
                      %mi b
                      %mo ↦
                      %mi μ
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      d_{\mathfrak{string}} : b \mapsto \mu
                      \\,.
          %p
            That the differential defined this way is indeed of degree +1 and squares to 0 is precisely the fact that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            is a degree 3-cocycle of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔤
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
          %p
            One can equivalently describe the
            = succeed "-algebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_\infty
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔰𝔱𝔯𝔦𝔫𝔤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{string}(n)
            in terms of lots of brackets
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mi k
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi k
                  %mi 𝔰𝔱𝔯𝔦𝔫𝔤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi 𝔰𝔱𝔯𝔦𝔫𝔤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  [-,-,\dots,-]_k:\wedge^k \mathfrak{string}(n)\to \mathfrak{string}(n),
          %p
            of degree
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                    %mo −
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2-k
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔤
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mn 3
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mo ∧
                    %mn 3
                  %mi 𝔤
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi b
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  [-,-,-]_3:\wedge^3 \mathfrak{g}\to \langle b\rangle^*
          %p given by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo ,
                  %mi z
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mn 3
                  %mo =
                  %mi μ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo ,
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mi β
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  [x,y,z]_3=\mu(x,y,z)\cdot \beta,
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi β
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta:\langle b\rangle\to\mathbb{R}
            is the dual of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
          %h4#strict_lie_2algebra_model Strict Lie 2-algebra model
          %p
            %strong Proposition
            The string Lie 2-algebra given above is equivalent to the infinite-dimensional Lie 2-algebra coming from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+crossed+module"} differential crossed module
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi Ω
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mi 𝔤
                  %mo →
                  %mi P
                  %mi 𝔤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\hat \Omega \mathfrak{g} \to P \mathfrak{g}
          %p
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+central+extension"} universal central extension
            of the
            %span.newWikiWord
              loop Lie algebra
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/loop+Lie+algebra"} ?
            mapping into the path Lie algebra, which acts on the former in the evident way.
          %p
            This is proven in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#BCSS"} BCSS
          %h3#AsHomotopyFiber As a homotopy fiber
          %p
            Up to equivalence, the string Lie 2-algebra is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+fiber"} homotopy fiber
            of the cocycle
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                    %mo :
                    %mi 𝔰𝔬
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi b
                      %mn 2
                    %mi 𝔲
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu : \mathfrak{so}(n) \to \mathbf{b}^2 \mathfrak{u}(1)
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi b
                    %mi 𝔲
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{b} \mathfrak{u}(1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+%E2%88%9E-bundle"} principal ∞-bundle
            over
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔰𝔬
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{so}(n)
          %p
            Here we take by definition the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
            of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Lie+algebroid"} ∞-Lie algebroid
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presentable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} presented
            by the opposite (after passing to
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chevalley-Eilenberg+algebras"} Chevalley-Eilenberg algebras
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+dg-algebras"} model structure on dg-algebras
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+fiber"} homotopy fiber
            see section 3.1 and specifically example 3.5.4 of (
            %a{:href => "#FiorenzaRogersSchreiber13"}> Fiorenza-Rogers-Schreiber 13
            ).
          %p In terms of dg-algebras, the cocycle is dually a morphism
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi CE
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔰𝔬
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ←
                  %mi CE
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi b
                    %mn 2
                  %mi 𝔲
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  CE(\mathfrak{so}(n)) \leftarrow CE(\mathbf{b}^2 \mathfrak{u}(1)) : \mu
          %p
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+fiber"} homotopy fiber
            in question is dually modeled by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+pushout"} homotopy pushout
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mn 0
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↑
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi CE
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi 𝔰𝔬
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi n
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo ←
                            %mi μ
                        %mtd
                          %mi CE
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msup
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                              %mi b
                            %mn 2
                          %mi 𝔲
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \&amp;&amp; 0
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp; \uparrow
                  \\\
                  CE(\mathfrak{so}(n)) &amp;\stackrel{\mu}{\leftarrow}&amp;
                  CE(\mathbf{b}^2 \mathfrak{u}(1))
                  }
                  \\,.
          %p
            By the general rules for computing
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+pushout"} homotopy pushout
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pushout"} pushout
            if we choose a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/resolution"} resolution
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi CE
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi b
                    %mn 2
                  %mi 𝔲
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CE(\mathbf{b}^2 \mathfrak{u}(1)) \to 0
            by a cofibration and ensure that all three objects in the pushout diagram are cofibrations.
          %p
            For the resolution we take the standard one by the CE-algebra of the
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi b
                      %mn 2
                    %mi 𝔲
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{b}^2 \mathfrak{u}(1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+principal+%E2%88%9E-bundle"} universal principal ∞-bundle
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mrow
                        %mi e
                        %mi b
                    %mi 𝔲
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{e b} \mathfrak{u}(1)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi CE
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mrow
                      %mi e
                      %mi b
                  %mi 𝔲
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mo ∧
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo ⊕
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  CE(\mathbf{e b} \mathfrak{u}(1)) = (\wedge^\bullet( \langle b\rangle \oplus \langle c\rangle ), d)
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
            is a generator of degree 2,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
            one of degree 3 and the differential is given by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mi b
                  %mo =
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  d b = c
          %p and
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mi c
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  d c = 0
                  \\,.
          %p
            The morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi CE
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi b
                    %mn 2
                  %mi 𝔲
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi CE
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi e
                  %mi b
                  %mi 𝔲
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CE(\mathbf{b}^2 \mathfrak{u}(1)) \to CE(e b \mathfrak{u}(1))
            is the one that identifies the two degree-3 generators.
          %p
            Now
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi CE
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi b
                    %mn 2
                  %mi 𝔲
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CE(\mathbf{b}^2 \mathfrak{u}(1))
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi CE
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mrow
                      %mi e
                      %mi b
                  %mi 𝔲
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CE(\mathbf{e b} \mathfrak{u}(1))
            are
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sullivan+algebra"} Sullivan algebra
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+dg-algebra"} model structure on dg-algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi CE
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CE(\mathfrak{g})
            is not quite a Sullivan algebra, but almost: it is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semifree+dga"} semifree dga
            and only fails to have the filtering property on the differential. This is sufficient for computing the desired
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+fiber"} homotopy fiber
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "infinity-Lie+algebra+cohomology#Extensions"} ∞-Lie algebra cohomology – Extensions
          %p One observes now that
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi CE
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi 𝔰𝔱𝔯𝔦𝔫𝔤
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mo ←
                        %mtd
                          %mi CE
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mrow
                              %mi e
                              %mi b
                          %mi 𝔲
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↑
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↑
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi CE
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi 𝔰𝔬
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi n
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mo ←
                        %mtd
                          %mi CE
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msup
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                              %mi b
                            %mn 2
                          %mi 𝔲
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  CE(\mathfrak{string}) &amp;\leftarrow&amp; CE(\mathbf{e b} \mathfrak{u}(1))
                  \\\
                  \\uparrow &amp;&amp; \uparrow
                  \\\
                  CE(\mathfrak{so}(n)) &amp;\leftarrow&amp; CE(\mathbf{b}^2 \mathfrak{u}(1))
                  }
          %p
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pushout"} pushout
            diagram. Dually, this exhibits
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔰𝔱𝔯𝔦𝔫𝔤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{string}
            as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-pullback"} (∞,1)-pullback
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi 𝔰𝔱𝔯𝔦𝔫𝔤
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi n
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mo *
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi 𝔰𝔬
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi n
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mi μ
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                              %mi b
                            %mn 2
                          %mi 𝔲
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \\mathfrak{string}(n) &amp;\to&amp; *
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow
                  \\\
                  \\mathfrak{so}(n) &amp;\stackrel{\mu}{\to}&amp; \mathbf{b}^2 \mathfrak{u}(1)
                  }
                  \\,.
          %p And this may be taken to be the abstract definition of the string Lie 2-algebra.
          %p
            By the general logic of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+sequence"} fiber sequences
            this implies that also
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi b
                  %mi 𝔲
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi 𝔰𝔱𝔯𝔦𝔫𝔤
                  %mo →
                  %mi 𝔰𝔬
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbf{b} \mathfrak{u}(1) \to \mathfrak{string} \to \mathfrak{so}(n)
          %p is a fiber sequence. By analogous reasoning as before, we see that this is modeled by the ordinary pushout
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi CE
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi b
                          %mi 𝔲
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mo ←
                        %mtd
                          %mo *
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↑
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↑
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi CE
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi 𝔰𝔱𝔯𝔦𝔫𝔤
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mo ←
                        %mtd
                          %mi CE
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi 𝔤
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  CE(\mathbf{b} \mathfrak{u}(1)) &amp;\leftarrow&amp; *
                  \\\
                  \\uparrow &amp;&amp; \uparrow
                  \\\
                  CE(\mathfrak{string}) &amp;\leftarrow&amp; CE(\mathfrak{g})
                  }
                  \\,.
          %p
            This is indeed a homotopy pushout even without resolving the point, because
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi CE
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔰𝔱𝔯𝔦𝔫𝔤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ←
                  %mi CE
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CE(\mathfrak{string}) \leftarrow CE(\mathfrak{g})
            is already a cofibration, being the pushout of a cofibration by the above.
          %h3#as_the_prequantum_line_2bundle_of_a_courant_algebroid As the prequantum line 2-bundle of a Courant algebroid
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delooping"} delooping
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semisimple+Lie+algebra"} semisimple Lie algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
            to a 1-object
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L-infinity+algebroid"} L-infinity algebroid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                  %mi 𝔤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \mathfrak{g}
            carries the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Killing+form"} Killing form
            as a quadratic bilinear
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/invariant+polynomial"} invariant polynomial
            and is as such a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+Lie+n-algebroid"} symplectic Lie n-algebroid
            over the point for
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo =
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = 2
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Courant+Lie+2-algebroid"} Courant Lie 2-algebroid
            over the point.
          %p
            As described at
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+infinity-groupoid"} symplectic infinity-groupoid
            one can consider the higher analog of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
            of these objects. This is again the homotopy fiber as
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "AsHomotopyFiber"} above
          %h3#as_a_heisenberg_lie_2algebra As a Heisenberg Lie 2-algebra
          %p
            The String Lie 2-algebra identifies also with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heisenberg+Lie+2-algebra"} Heisenberg Lie 2-algebra
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string"} string
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"} sigma-model
            for the specialization to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/WZW+model"} WZW model
            (
            %a{:href => "#BaezRogers10"}> Baez-Rogers 10
            ). See at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-plectic+geometry"} 2-plectic geometry
            for more.
          %h2#generalizations Generalizations
          %p
            More generally, for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
            an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L-infinity-algebra"} ∞-Lie algebra
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi CE
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu \in CE(\mathfrak{g})
            an
            = succeed "-Lie" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chevalley-Eilenberg+algebra"} Chevalley-Eilenberg algebra
            of
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔤
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi b
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mo −
                      %mn 2
                  %mi 𝔲
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mi μ
                  %mo →
                  %mi 𝔤
                  %mo →
                  %mn 0
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  0 \to \mathbf{b}^{k-2} \mathfrak{u}(1) \to \mathfrak{g}_\mu  \to \mathfrak{g} \to 0
                  \\,.
          %p
            For instance the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity+Lie+3-algebra"} supergravity Lie 3-algebra
            is such an extension of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Poincare+Lie+algebra"} super Poincare Lie algebra
            by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Lie+algebra"} super Lie algebra
            4-cocycle.
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/special+orthogonal+Lie+algebra"} special orthogonal Lie algebra
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fivebrane+Lie+6-algebra"} fivebrane Lie 6-algebra
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+II+supergravity+Lie+2-algebra"} type II supergravity Lie 2-algebra
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity+Lie+3-algebra"}> supergravity Lie 3-algebra
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity+Lie+6-algebra"} supergravity Lie 6-algebra
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+Lie+algebroid"} string Lie algebroid
          %h2#references References
          %p
            In one incarnation or other the String Lie 2-algebra has been considered in literature of
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dg-algebra"} dg-algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+theory"} Lie theoretic
            interpretation as a Lie 2-algebra has been made fully explicit only in
          %ul
            %li#BaezCrans03
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Baez"}> John Baez
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alissa+Crans"} Alissa Crans
              %em Theory and Applications of Categories
              %strong 12
              (2004), 492-528. (
              %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/volumes/12/15/12-15abs.html"}> web
              )
              %p
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math.QA/0307263"}> arXiv:math.QA/0307263
                )
          %p In
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andre+Henriques"}> Andre Henriques
              ,
              %em
                Integrating
                = succeed "-algebras" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi L
                          %mn ∞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_\infty
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0603563"}> arXiv:0603563
              )
          %p
            the string Lie 2-algebra is integrated to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+2-group"} string 2-group
            using the general abstract method described at
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+integration"} Lie integration
          %p In
          %ul
            %li#BCSS
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Baez"}> John Baez
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alissa+Crans"} Alissa Crans
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"} Urs Schreiber
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Danny+Stevenson"} Danny Stevenson
              %em Homotopy, Homology and Applications
              %strong 9
              (2007), 101-135. &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math.QA/0504123"}> arXiv:math.QA/0504123
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://projecteuclid.org/journals/homology-homotopy-and-applications/volume-9/issue-2/From-loop-groups-to-2-groups/hha/1201127333.full"} hha:1201127333
          %p
            the equivalent strict model given by a differential crossed module is found, which is then integrated termwise as ordinary Lie algebras to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/crossed+module"} crossed module
            of Frechet-Lie groups, hence to a Lie
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+2-group"} strict 2-group
            model of the String Lie 2-group.
          %p
            The string Lie 2-algebra as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heisenberg+Lie+2-algebra"} Heisenberg Lie 2-algebra
            on the group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            is discussed in
          %ul
            %li#BaezRogers10
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Baez"}> John Baez
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chris+Rogers"} Chris Rogers
              = succeed "," do
                %em Categorified Symplectic Geometry and the String Lie 2-Algebra
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0901.4721"} arXiv:0901.4721
              = succeed ")." do
                %a{:href => "https://projecteuclid.org/euclid.hha/1296223828"} euclid
          %p The string Lie 2-algebra is also considered in a certain context in
          %ul
            %li
              Sati, Schreiber, Stasheff,
              %em
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi L
                        %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_\infty
                \-algebra connections
          %p
            where also the relation to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity+Lie+3-algebra"} supergravity Lie 3-algebra
            and other structures is discussed.
          %p
            The super-
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_\infty
            \-version of the string
            = succeed "-algebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_\infty
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Baez"}> John Baez
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Huerta"} John Huerta
              %em Division Algebras and Supersymmetry II
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1003.3436"}> arXiv:1003.3436
              ).
          %p
            See also
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/division+algebra+and+supersymmetry"} division algebra and supersymmetry
          %p Discussion of the string Lie 2-algebra as the homotopy fiber of the underlying 3-cocycle is around prop. 3.3.96 in
          %ul
            %li
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/differential+cohomology+in+a+cohesive+topos"} differential cohomology in a cohesive topos
          %p and example 3.5.4 in
          %ul
            %li#FiorenzaRogersSchreiber13
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Domenico+Fiorenza"}> Domenico Fiorenza
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chris+Rogers"} Chris Rogers
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"} Urs Schreiber
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/L-%E2%88%9E+algebras+of+local+observables+from+higher+prequantum+bundles"} L-∞ algebras of local observables from higher prequantum bundles
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1304.6292"}> arXiv:1304.6292
              )
          %p More on this in
          %ul
            %li#Schmidt19
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lennart+Schmidt"}> Lennart Schmidt
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Twisted Weil Algebras for the String Lie 2-Algebra
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Christian+Saemann"} Christian Saemann
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"} Urs Schreiber
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin+Wolf"} Martin Wolf
              (eds.)
              %em
                %a{:href => "http://www.maths.dur.ac.uk/lms/109/index.html"} Higher Structures in M-Theory
              %a{:href => "http://www.maths.dur.ac.uk/lms/"} Durham Symposium
              2018, Fortschritte der Physik 2019 (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1903.02873"}> arXiv:1903.02873
              )
        .revisedby
